 ("R-409",4,"Lighting","HDR bloom & tonemapping for fireworks","P1","done","L","Optional HDR pipeline with bloom; off = original look","R-408","SDK patch 0004: Glow and HDR settings in Video Settings, both live. Glow = quarter-size bloom (bright pass, three blurs) that leaves out the HUD via a mask built from the UI quad list; HDR = scRGB swap chain with highlights expanded. Both confirmed looking good in play 2026-11-11. Windows Auto HDR accepted 2026-11-11 pending issues in testing. Next: true HDR rendering (float render target). See docs/GRAPHICS.md. 2026-11-12: starting true HDR rendering. SDK patch 0008 logs each colour target format, its multisampling and each copy to a texture ('[bbrr] render target ...' / '[bbrr] resolve ...'); these are the unknowns GRAPHICS.md lists. Built, not run. Marked done 2026-11-12 at the user's decision: Glow and HDR output meet the acceptance and already look good; true HDR rendering judged a modest gain for a large, risky change and moved to R-907 (post-1.0). Patch 0008's logging stays for the anti-aliasing work (R-405)."),
 ("R-410",4,"Lighting","Improved city lighting","P2","done","L","Firework light reflected on city (dynamic point lights), optional","R-408","Researched 2026-11-11 (docs/GRAPHICS.md): city is texture x one scene-wide ambient colour, no normals; plan = per-firework light list + replaced city shader. Ray tracing only as a later hybrid (shadows/reflections); RTX Remix not applicable. 2026-11-12: started on branch city-lighting (tag pre-city-lighting marks the version before it; that build's exe and DLLs are copied to out/backup-pre-city-lighting). Chosen route: per-building tint - each city draw gets its own ambient colour from nearby fireworks - instead of per-pixel lighting, which needs shader replacement (R-408). Step 1, built, not run: a probe (--city_light_probe, src/city_lighting.cpp, SDK patch 0009) that logs every draw of one frame 12 s and 25 s into a song (shader constants, vertex box), the first firework bursts and spark emitters. Nothing on screen changes yet. Probe results 2026-11-12 (two runs): the city's pixel shaders (238C, C9D2, 88B6) already light per pixel from one point light - position c15, colour c16, strength c17.x, inverse-square falloff - while the game keeps six firework lights and gives them all only to the smoke shader (vertex shader BBFF: positions c8-c13, colour and strength c16-c21). City pieces are world-space draws, about 1,000 small ones plus a few hundred chunks. Step 2, built, not run: SDK patch 0011 gives every city draw its own stand-in light for all six fireworks as seen from that piece; setting City Lighting (Off / Low / Medium / High / Very High = strength 1, 2, 4, 8; --city_lighting) in Video Settings, off by default. '[bbrr] city light' log lines show the game's own values next to ours. Also learned: the main picture is drawn without multisampling (for R-405). First test 2026-11-12: works and looks better than the original (colours more pronounced, the flash spreads over buildings more naturally); Low almost imperceptible, Very High quite strong; seams between pieces; rooftops not bright enough. The log showed the first version's brightness followed the game's own flash (c17.x starts at 500,000 and dies within frames) instead of the lights' own strengths. Second version (SDK patch 0012), built, not run: fixed brightness scale (strength 1 = the game's flash at its peak, lasting as long as the game's smoke lights do); every piece lit from one shared centre so the falloff is continuous (large pieces get the total colour, small ones the mix arriving at them); flat faces brighter when facing the light (the shader ignores surface direction for this light); the game's own flash kept on top; new top level Extreme (32). Second version confirmed in play 2026-11-12 with screenshots: 'looks great', significantly improved; Medium and High look best. The top level is over the top but kept at the user's request, renamed Acid Trip. Still on branch city-lighting, not merged to main. Later the same day: a brightness ceiling was added (patches 0013-0016) because walls near a burst or a rising rocket washed out to white: the light is kept at least 350 units from each piece's box and the piece's nearest point gets no more than the ceiling; light colours get a share of white so textures still show. The user tried ceilings in play and chose 0.35 (0.5 and above blow out; small patches of strong colour remain at 0.35, accepted for now - probably wide blocks, which cannot be bounded this way). Measured cost on the user's PC: 0.10-0.15 ms a frame. Default is now Medium, ceiling 0.35 (--city_light_ceiling). scripts/build.ps1 now copies the SDK's DLLs every time (an SDK-only change was not reaching the game). Marked done and merged into main 2026-11-12 at the user's request. Possible follow-up, not planned: a rough 3D map of the city so the ceiling also holds on wide blocks."),
 ("R-411",4,"Particles","Particle quality/quantity scaling","P1","done","M","Multiplier on firework Count with stable perf","R-302","Particle Density in Video Settings (--particle_quality, 0.25x-5x multiplier, live): scales each firework's Count at config load and rewrites loaded configs when changed. Game stops making sparks at 70000 alive. Running at the game's own 70000 limit crashed twice (2026-11-11), so the port stops adding sparks at 68000 (--particle_limit) and the setting tops out at 5x. Full song at 5x with that limit confirmed without a crash; both values locked in. Keep the [particles] log lines in place: they are how a spark-related crash will be recognised later (ends with ShaderTranslator 'Unsupported write to export' errors). 2026-11-12: 0x added (no sparks at all: the spark-adding functions are skipped; counts stay as in the files). Untested. 0x confirmed working 2026-11-12. Marked done 2026-11-12."),
