 ("R-806",8,"Feature","Average timing on the results screen","P2","done","M","Results screen shows mean hit offset (ms, early/late) and spread for the song just played; optional per-difficulty history","R-801,R-803","Done 2026-11-11: results screen line with average, spread, early and late counts (see R-803). Per-difficulty history not done."),
 ("R-807",8,"Feature","Offset suggestion on the results screen","P2","done","S","When a song ends with a clearly one-sided result (most presses early, or most late, and an average well away from zero), the results screen suggests which way and roughly how far to move Input Offset; nothing is shown for a balanced result or too few hits","R-806","Requested 2026-11-11 after raising Input Offset by about 40 ms fixed a steady -36 to -44 ms late pattern. Thresholds to settle when building (for example 20+ hits, average beyond 15 ms, 70% on one side). Left out of R-806 at first on purpose. Built 2026-11-11, shown working in a screenshot the same day (avg +26 ms early at +20 -> try -5): bbrr.hit.advice in src/input_offset.cpp writes STR118 for ui_scripts/Ingame/LevelBreakdown.lua when 20+ hits, average 15+ ms off and 70%+ on that side; suggests current offset - average, rounded to 5 ms, within +-100 ms; shown even with Hit Timing off; one [input] offset advice log line per results screen. Wording changed on request: '(Current setting +20 ms)' instead of '(now +20 ms)'. 2026-11-12: changed at the user's request to give a direction only, no target number ('Mostly early. Try lowering Input Offset a little (currently +15 ms)'): one song's average is too rough to aim at and a number invites obsessing; players fine-tune over several songs. This also removes the '-0 ms' the old text could show. Untested. Direction-only wording approved by the user and locked in 2026-11-12; marked done."),
 ("R-901",9,"Accessibility","Reduce background/camera motion option","P2","done","M","Setting (Off/Reduced/Static) that damps or freezes the city camera movement from the *_SplCamera splines without affecting gameplay or note timing","R-104","Stretch goal: background movement can be disorienting (user feedback 2026-11-10) 2026-11-12: built as Modifiers > Camera Speed (100 down to 0 in steps of 10, --camera_speed, src/camera_speed.cpp). The camera, launch and burst points follow three looping splines per song (_SplCamera, _SplLaunch, _SplTarget), loaded by sub_82086948 and read by sub_82085F08 at song time; on those three the song time is multiplied by the speed, so the whole scene travels less far and rockets still reach the target line on time. Applies from the next song. Possible side effect: below 100 the closing camera move (_Out spline) may jump. Built, not run. Confirmed working by the user 2026-11-12 and marked done; menu order changed so Left lowers it. TO TEST during the full playthrough (R-110): every song at a few speeds (100, 50, 0), the closing camera move at song end, the menu background."),
 ("R-902",9,"Research","Accessibility review","P2","todo","S","docs/ACCESSIBILITY.md: motion, flashing/brightness of fireworks, colour-blind-safe note colours, UI/text scaling, one-handed/remappable controls; prioritised list","R-104","Stretch goal"),
 ("R-903",9,"Feature","VR support","P2","todo","XL","Play in a VR headset (OpenXR): stereo rendering of the city and fireworks with head tracking, menus and HUD on a comfortable panel, timing unaffected","R-402,R-302","Stretch goal (requested 2026-11-10). Needs per-eye camera/projection hooks and a stereo present path in the SDK"),
 ("R-904",9,"Feature","Gameplay across the full screen width","P2","todo","L","On wide screens the rockets, arrows and target line use the whole width of the display instead of the middle 16:9 part; judging unchanged","R-402,R-415","Stretch goal (requested 2026-11-11, experimental). Needs: how launch/target splines and zones map to screen positions, and whether they can be spread by aspect ratio without changing charts."),
 ("R-905",9,"Feature","Visualizer driven by audio playing in Windows","P2","done","L","Visualizer mode reacts to whatever is playing on the PC (system audio capture), no game music needed","R-211","Stretch goal (requested 2026-11-11, experimental). Idea: WASAPI loopback capture feeding the visualizer's beat/level input; first find what the original visualizer reads (XMP / console music levels). 2026-11-12, built, untested (src/visualizer.cpp): how the original works: entering the Visualizer (sub_8207D250 state 2) calls XMPCaptureOutput (game wrapper sub_8215EFA0, XMP msg 0x7003D) with callback sub_82076F68; the console music player hands it 256 stereo frames (512 big-endian floats) at a time; the callback takes the level, an FFT and finds beats. Now: the wrapper is answered by the port, WASAPI loopback captures the PC's sound (process loopback leaving the game out, Windows 10 2004+; --visualizer_include_game for plain loopback), and the audio is handed to the callback on the game thread at the start of each game update (sub_8207D5F0). Seen working 2026-11-12 (music peaks 0.28-0.47). Then, untested: Visualizer Sensitivity (BBRR Settings, --visualizer_sensitivity 0.5/1/2/4): the music is level-matched (slow-falling peak brought to 0.8) and multiplied by the sensitivity before the game sees it. Tested: gain 1.5x vs 12x made no visible difference (the game finds beats relative to the music around them). Now, untested: sensitivity reshapes the dynamics instead: each 256-frame chunk scaled by (rms / 0.35 s average)^(e - 1), e = 0.35 / 1 / 1.6 / 2.2. 2026-11-12: sensitivity also scales the launcher's interval (game state 0x821A0578 +260) and level (+268) while the Visualizer runs, restored after; this gave a clear difference. Parked as complete."),
 ("R-906",9,"Feature","Custom charts and automatic chart generation","P2","todo","XL","A documented chart format and a tool to build a pack from the player's own music; an auto-generator proposes a playable chart from an audio file (beat/onset detection) that can then be edited by hand","R-111,R-112","Stretch goal (requested 2026-11-11, experimental). Charts are tab-separated text (.lvl: BPM, length, offset, target width, launch/detonation rows) plus spline files; packs are folders with contents.txt, so home-made packs look feasible. Needs: music encoding the game accepts, pack layout without a console package, and a generator."),
 ("R-907",9,"Lighting","True HDR rendering (float render target)","P2","todo","XL","The game's colour target keeps brightness above white (16-bit float) through to presentation, so overlapping fireworks keep their colour and gradation instead of clipping; tone-mapped for SDR screens; every existing graphics feature still looks the same","R-409","Stretch goal, split from R-409 on 2026-11-12. Expected gain is modest (visible mainly in the cores of overlapping fireworks in busy sections) and the change touches how the emulated GPU stores every pixel (render target cache, resolve/copy path, read-back texture, presentation), so everything graphical would need retesting. See docs/GRAPHICS.md, HDR level 3. SDK patch 0008 logs the render target formats and multisampling it needs to know first."),
 ("R-801",8,"Research","Research modern rhythm-game features","P1","todo","M","docs/RHYTHM_FEATURES.md: survey (speed mods, timing feedback, measure/beat lines, etc.), feasibility vs .lvl beat data + engine hooks, prioritised list","R-109","Requested 2026-11-10"),
 ("R-802",8,"Feature","Note speed / scroll-speed modifier","P2","done","M","Adjustable approach speed independent of song tempo; judging unchanged","R-801","Modifiers page in BBRR Settings with Scroll Speed 0.5x-4x (--scroll_speed): each note's flight time is divided by the multiplier when the chart loads (hook on sub_82065E70, src/modifiers.cpp), so arrows launch later and travel faster along the same path. Confirmed working in play 2026-11-11. Now also live: RB / LB step it up / down during a song (notes not yet launched are retimed, arrows in the air are repositioned every frame; hooks on sub_82064C50 and sub_8206A328), with a brief on-screen notice. First live build launched new arrows late (fixed: the rewritten launch time must be before 'now'); fix accepted 2026-11-11 pending issues in testing. Song select screen: speed panel right of the list with LB / RB, and each bar shows BPM x speed (right) and length (left, smaller font); mid-song change and bar layout confirmed in play 2026-11-11. Follow-ups (accepted 2026-11-11 pending issues in testing): first notes of a song get their full path back when the speed allows (the game builds them part-way up); panel moved right with LB / RB icons (confirmed in a screenshot); 'Length' and 'BPM' headings level with the screen title. 2026-11-11 (2): first arrow of a song still appeared late at higher speeds (log: its flight stayed at the shortened 1.6 s). The full path is now taken from any full-length note with the same end whose path runs through the shortened start (the old rule also required the same note kind, which first notes rarely share); each attempt is logged. Untested. 2026-11-11 (3): still 'no full path found' for the first notes (each note has its own end). Second way added: the full start is where the line from the end through the shortened start meets the plane of the songs' launch points (the start coordinate that varies least), flight = path length / the song's rocket speed. Untested. 2026-11-12: choices 0.25x to 4x in steps of 0.25 (16 steps). At 0.25x and 0.5x early arrows went missing; fix: a retimed launch is never put before the song start; confirmed working at 0.25x 2026-11-12. Speeds below 1x now retime every waiting note at load and on each LB/RB step (RetimeWaiting), since the game only checks a note once its launch time has come. Marked done 2026-11-12 (first-arrow fixes accepted pending issues in testing). 2026-11-12: holding RB / LB keeps stepping (first repeat after 0.4 s, then every 0.15 s), during songs and on song select (requested)."),
 ("R-803",8,"Feature","Timing feedback (early/late, ms offset)","P2","done","M","Per-hit early/late indicator and end-of-song timing histogram / mean offset","R-801,R-306","Per-hit +/- ms readout next to the arrow (Hit Timing) and a results-screen line with average, spread (standard deviation), early and late counts, centred below the results box. Position and numbers confirmed in play 2026-11-11; readout raised to 40 px above the target line so fireworks do not bury it, position locked in. Left: two-player results screen; optional histogram. Setting renamed 'Show Accuracy' (was 'Hit Timing') 2026-11-11; cvar still --hit_timing. Tracker clean-up 2026-11-12: marked done. Not built: the optional histogram and a two-player results line."),
 ("R-804",9,"Feature","Beat guides (measure lines / timing bars) - dropped","P2","done","M","Not planned: kept only as a record of what was tried","R-801","Dropped 2026-11-11 at the user's request. Tried: measure lines on the beat grid (cannot follow arrows: every note has its own flight time, 3.2-5.7 s in one song, and arrows ease along their paths, so arrows on one beat are only level at the target line), then a bar through every arrow (Timing Bars), which the user did not like. Code removed. Tracker clean-up 2026-11-12: closed (dropped earlier at the user's request; it was still counted as to do)."),
 ("R-808",8,"Feature","Background setting (practice)","P2","done","S","Modifiers option that blacks out the city behind the playfield during a song; arrows, fireworks and HUD unchanged","R-801","Built 2026-11-11, untested: --no_background. SDK patch 0005 lets the port hide pixel shaders by the shader file they come from; the city is the only thing drawn with TextureModDiffuse.xpu, so its draws are skipped while game state is playing/failing. Street lights and smoke are point sprites and stay. The same SDK hook is a first step towards shader replacement by hash (R-408). 2026-11-11 first test: did nothing (log: city shader registered, never matched). The microcode in GPU memory has every word in the opposite byte order to the .xpu file and D3D renumbers a few registers when the shader is created (city: 10 of 12 words equal, hash 9567C79307ACC6F5). Matching is now 80% of words equal, in the right byte order; checked offline against the shader cache: only the city shader matches. Untested in game. 2026-11-11 (2): still no effect. The shader cache stores microcode in guest byte order, which is the file's byte order; the emulator's host dwords are those bytes read big-endian. The previous fix compared the wrong way round. Now: file words read big-endian, 80% of words equal (city 10 of 12, checked offline in the same terms). Untested. 2026-11-11 (3): the third build hid the arrows instead of the city: 9567C79307ACC6F5 is a generic textured shader from SimpleShaders.fxobj (12 of 12 words), used by the arrows; the city's shader matches none of the game's shader files. Approach changed: patch 0005 now hides draws by (vertex, pixel) shader hash pair, and the first time No Background is on in a song it logs every draw of two frames ('[bbrr] draw list ...') to identify the city's draws. Until those are filled in (kCityDraws in src/modifiers.cpp) the setting has no visible effect. 2026-11-11 (4): draw list read (two frames, ~1,500 draws each). Hidden now: sky 0C88/0AA0, backdrop BBFF/9567, city mesh passes C837/2B08 and 04F2/C9D2 with 6773/88B6, city details D1ED/238C; frames start with clears, so the space behind stays black. Arrows (436F/9567), fireworks (PointSprite 1DB9/C471, points 63B4/4B1E), glow passes and HUD untouched. List overridable with --no_background_draws. Untested. 2026-11-11 (5): city gone in play, sky still faintly visible; added sky dome 04F2/B36F and water quad B90D/FB9F to the list. Confirmed working in play 2026-11-11 (city, sky and water gone). Then: hidden from the Get Ready countdown on (screen scripts Ingame\\GetReady / Hud start a song; LevelComplete, LevelBreakdown, GameOver, RetryMenu, HighScore, FireworkUnlocked, PostGame or Main\\ end it), not only once the song plays; untested. 2026-11-12: renamed Background with Default / Off (Off hides the city); confirmed working."),
 ("R-809",8,"Feature","Rocket Acceleration setting","P2","done","S","Modifiers > Rocket Acceleration: Default (the game's own movement) / Static (arrows move at one even speed)","R-801","Built 2026-11-11, untested: --rocket_acceleration. sub_8206A328 eases arrows with (flown/flight)^1.5 across and ^1.05 upwards; a mid-asm hook at 0x8206A418 (where the eased and linear branches meet) sets both fractions to flown/flight when off. The game's own test byte 0x821A01C4 must not be written: it is the top byte of a value used elsewhere (0x8099FFF0 in the image) - writing it in the first attempt made acceleration flip unpredictably. Seen working 2026-11-11 (hard to tell by eye). The setting is read every frame, so it applies mid-song: arrows in the air move to their even-pace position at once (mostly a small sideways shift). 2026-11-12: reported reversed in play (On looked steady, Off looked accelerating - the original movement reads as steady on screen). Swapped: Off (default) = the game's own movement, On = the even pace along the path, which looks like speeding up. Then renamed at the user's request: values Default (game's own movement) and Static (even speed the whole way); cvar --rocket_acceleration false/true. Confirmed working; choices renamed Default / Static."),
 ("R-810",8,"Feature","Horizontal Drift setting","P2","done","M","Modifiers > Horizontal Drift Default (original: arrows drift sideways towards their firework) / Off (straight up, in the screen column they first appear in)","R-801","Built 2026-11-12, untested: --horizontal_drift. Mid-asm hook at 0x8206A4C8 (sub_8206A328, rocket's new position at r1+80 before it is stored): x is solved from the game's view matrix (game + 0x103B0..0x103E0, used by the arrow icons) so the rocket stays in its launch point's screen column; height and depth unchanged, so it meets the target line on time; trail, arrow and firework follow the rocket. Whether the matrix is applied as p x M or M x p is checked against the icons' clip coordinates (log line '[modifiers] arrow view matrix applied as ...'). Confirmed working 2026-11-12. 2026-11-12 rework: the screen column from the launch point drifted when the camera swung (Ode to Joy); solving world x for a fixed column then ran away (arrows flew about). Now: the rocket keeps its firework's x and z, then is slid along the camera's left-right direction (gradient of clip x) to the screen column it first appeared in, checked per frame. Choices Default / Off. Confirmed working on Ode to Joy 2026-11-12."),
 ("R-811",8,"Feature","Autoplay","P2","done","M","Modifiers > Autoplay: every rocket hit at 0 ms (Perfect); a song in which it was on at any moment (countdown to end) saves no score locally or to leaderboards","R-801","Built 2026-11-12, untested: src/autoplay.cpp. Presses go through the game's own controller read (sub_820E6E50, XamInputGetState wrapper): the due rocket's button is added for one read; the judging hook judges it at the rocket's time. Button per rocket byte +8: first guess 0=A 1=B 2=X 3=Y (the game's colour order Green, Red, Blue, Yellow), self-corrects when a press is not judged, kept in cache/autoplay_buttons.txt. Scores: leaderboard writes ignored for that song; results-screen profile save (GUI.save_needed) skipped for the rest of the run. Note: the game's save does not reach disk yet anyway (R-107). 2026-11-12 first test: no presses at all (no button table written). User confirmed arrow colour = button (green A, red B, blue X, yellow Y). Second build: no filter on button value or player byte (any value learns its own button, first guess value % 4 = A, B, X, Y), plus one-off log lines: the first rockets' bytes 0-11, and pad reads / rockets tracked / presses in the first 5 s of play. Second test: still no automatic hits; the user's own presses all read 0.00. Log: ~190 pad reads a second (more than one per frame: menus and gameplay both read), so a button held for one read was usually missed by the gameplay read; rocket byte 8 is 0/2/4/6 (byte 1 the matching bit). Third build: byte 8 / 2 -> A, B, X, Y (colour order), buttons held 35 ms with a 30 ms gap, only Autoplay's own presses judged at 0 ms, self-learning removed. Third build confirmed working 2026-11-12. Confirmed working 2026-11-12. 2026-11-12 (later), untested: playing along gave non-zero timings because the player's own press could judge a rocket before Autoplay's (not stale input); now the player's A/B/X/Y are removed from the controller read while the song clock moves (pause menu unaffected). Achievements blocked too: hook on XUserWriteAchievements (sub_820E7478, XGI message 0xB0008) sends a count of 0 when Autoplay was used in the song. 2026-11-12 (evening): Autoplay did not press anything when the song was played from the second controller: presses only ever went to controller slot 0. Now they go to the slot that plays (UIGlobals.primary_pad, reported by ui_scripts/Ingame/Hud.lua) and, in two-player modes, to each rocket's own player (rocket byte 11). Built, not run. Still to test: that a song played with Autoplay saves no leaderboard score. 2026-11-12 (evening, Leaderboards Test on): Autoplay from the second controller now presses (user-confirmed) and the score was not saved, but (1) a firework was unlocked and (2) the results screen ignored every button: when the game ends its Xbox Live session it only lets go of the controller once the profile save has started, and Autoplay skips that save (it would have waited 60 s). Fixes, built, not run: the judging function's 'is this firework unlocked already' question (sub_8207A6E0 called from 0x82066B14) is answered yes in an Autoplay song, so nothing unlocks; LevelBreakdown.lua marks the skipped save as started (GUI.started_save) so the screen carries on. Confirmed 2026-11-12: no unlock and the results screen carries on. The 'new high score' screen still appeared after an Autoplay song; ui_scripts/Ingame/LevelComplete.lua now clears UIGlobals.show_highscore_achieved in an Autoplay song (bbrr.autoplay.song). Not run."),
 ("R-812",8,"Feature","DDR Mode","P2","done","S","Modifiers > DDR Mode: arrows climb straight up in four centred lanes by button, left to right Left (X), Down (A), Up (Y), Right (B); same button on one beat shares a lane","R-810","Built 2026-11-12, untested: --ddr_mode, lane spacing --ddr_lane_spacing (0.16 of half the screen width). Uses the Horizontal Drift Off hook (0x8206A4C8) with the lane's screen column instead of the first-seen column; works whatever Horizontal Drift is set to. Lane from rocket byte 8 / 2 (A, B, X, Y). 2026-11-12: seen working; lane spacing halved to 0.08."),
